The ideal of a filter in the ring system consists of the ring elements a such that I of the singleton a belongs to the filter. Zero belongs by upward closure from the master to I of the singleton zero, since zero lies in the ideal generated by every finite set. A sum belongs by closure under intersection, which rewrites the two singleton neighbourhoods as the neighbourhood of their union, followed by upward closure to the singleton of the sum; the inclusion criterion applies because the sum lies in the ideal generated by the two elements. A scalar multiple belongs by upward closure from the singleton neighbourhood to the singleton of the multiple, since the multiple lies in the ideal generated by the element.
